WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.314
AWS CLI output is often consumed by later
automation.

2
00:00:05.394 --> 00:00:11.384
A script may query JSON, extract an
identifier, select a resource,

3
00:00:11.464 --> 00:00:18.738
compare configuration, write a file, or
decide whether the next step should run.

4
00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:25.664
That output may contain resource names,
account details, ARNs, endpoints,

5
00:00:25.744 --> 00:00:31.628
policy documents, configuration values, or
sensitive context.

6
00:00:31.708 --> 00:00:38.661
Output handling should be deliberate
because later automation may trust it.

7
00:00:38.741 --> 00:00:44.196
Structured parsing is safer than fragile
text assumptions.

8
00:00:44.276 --> 00:00:51.978
Scripts should request expected output
formats and parse specific fields instead of

9
00:00:52.058 --> 00:00:55.481
depending on incidental formatting.

10
00:00:55.561 --> 00:01:02.086
They should handle empty responses,
multiple matches, missing fields,

11
00:01:02.166 --> 00:01:06.231
pagination, and unexpected values clearly.

12
00:01:06.311 --> 00:01:10.162
A missing result is not the same as
success,

13
00:01:10.242 --> 00:01:16.981
and multiple results may indicate that the
target was not specific enough.

14
00:01:17.061 --> 00:01:24.549
Output files should go to approved
destinations with appropriate permissions and

15
00:01:24.629 --> 00:01:26.983
retention expectations.

16
00:01:27.063 --> 00:01:33.374
Temporary files, generated JSON, policy
documents, inventory data,

17
00:01:33.454 --> 00:01:40.942
and support bundles can reveal sensitive
operational context even when they do not

18
00:01:41.022 --> 00:01:43.375
contain raw credentials.

19
00:01:43.455 --> 00:01:50.194
The script should avoid writing sensitive
output into shared workspaces,

20
00:01:50.274 --> 00:01:55.730
broad CI/CD artifacts, or long-lived logs
without a reason.

21
00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:59.982
Logs should provide minimal useful
evidence.

22
00:02:00.062 --> 00:02:07.336
Good logs show high-level identity,
account, region, action, target category,

23
00:02:07.416 --> 00:02:09.020
time, and result.

24
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:14.877
They avoid access keys, session tokens,
secrets, private data,

25
00:02:14.957 --> 00:02:21.696
excessive raw responses, and sensitive
policy or configuration details.

26
00:02:21.776 --> 00:02:29.585
Secure output handling helps automation
remain useful without turning logs and files

27
00:02:29.665 --> 00:02:32.660
into another source of exposure.
